Cache side-channel attacks such as Flush+Reload and Prime+Probe continue to threaten isolation across mutually untrusted processes. Although many attack detection mechanisms have been proposed, recent Intel processors introduce non-inclusive last-level caches and directory-based coherence. These architectural changes give rise to new attack behaviors that have not been examined by previous detection methods, which predominantly focus on inclusive cache hierarchies. As a result, real-time techniques that can account for both flush-based and directory-based eviction patterns without relying on assumptions specific to individual attack types remain largely underexplored. In this study, we introduce Abortection, a real-time detection framework for cache side-channel attacks on modern Intel processors using Intel TSX. We first present a systematic analysis of TSX Restricted Transactional Memory from the perspective of cache security, and show that transactional abort behavior provides a stable indicator of adversarial eviction activity, including behaviors induced by non-inclusive cache hierarchies and directory-based coherence. Building on this insight, Abortection detects various cache attacks in a unified manner across both instruction-triggered and prime-based eviction patterns, without requiring attack-specific assumptions or per-attack customization. We evaluate Abortection on Intel Xeon processors with non-inclusive caches under realistic workloads. Instruction-based attacks (Flush+Reload, Flush+Flush, Demote+Reload) achieve over 99% detection accuracy across noise conditions from 0% to 90%. For directory-based Prime+Probe, Abortection achieves at least 98% accuracy under noise up to 40%, and maintains 92% accuracy even at the highest noise level. These results demonstrate that monitoring fundamental transactional abort patterns provides an efficient and robust foundation for detecting diverse cache side-channel threats on modern processors.